steps.body:
To climb `n` stairs taking 1 or 2 steps at a time, the last move came from stair `n - 1` or from stair `n - 2`, so `ways(n) = ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2)`. As plain recursion, that calls `ways(n - 2)` twice, `ways(n - 3)` three times, and so on: the calls grow exponentially. A **memo** saves each answer the first time, so every `n` is computed once:

```cpp
long long ways(int n, std::vector<long long>& memo) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return 1;                    // one way: stay put, or take one step
    }
    if (memo[n] != -1) {
        return memo[n];              // solved already
    }
    memo[n] = ways(n - 1, memo) + ways(n - 2, memo);
    return memo[n];
}

std::vector<long long> memo(51, -1);
std::cout << ways(50, memo) << '\n';   // 20365011074
```

Each answer only needs smaller ones, so you can also fill them from the bottom up in a **table**, with no recursion at all:

```cpp
int n = 50;
std::vector<long long> dp(n + 1, 0);
dp[0] = 1;
dp[1] = 1;
for (int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) {
    dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2];
}
std::cout << dp[n] << '\n';   // 20365011074
```

Both are O(n). Use `long long`, since 50 stairs already have over 20 billion ways, and size the table `n + 1` so `dp[n]` exists. -1 works as the memo's "not computed yet" because no real answer is negative.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

long long countWays(int n) {
    std::vector<long long> dp(n + 1, 0);
    dp[0] = 1;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        dp[i] = dp[i - 1];
        if (i >= 2) {
            dp[i] += dp[i - 2];
        }
    }
    return dp[n];
}

int main() {
    int q = 0;
    std::cin >> q;
    for (int t = 0; t < q; ++t) {
        int n = 0;
        std::cin >> n;
        std::cout << n << " stairs: " << countWays(n) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

