steps.body:
House robber: each house holds some money, and you can't take from two houses side by side. For each house, choose: **skip** it and keep the best from the houses before it, or **take** it and add its money to the best from the houses before its neighbor. Let `best[i]` be the most you can take from the first `i` houses:

```cpp
std::vector<int> houses = {2, 7, 9, 3, 1};
int n = static_cast<int>(houses.size());
std::vector<long long> best(n + 1, 0);   // best[0]: no houses, nothing to take
best[1] = houses[0];
for (int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) {
    best[i] = std::max(best[i - 1], best[i - 2] + houses[i - 1]);   // skip, or take
}
std::cout << best[n] << '\n';   // 12, from 2 + 9 + 1
```

`best[i]` covers the first `i` houses, so `houses[i - 1]` is the newest one. That shift by one lets `best[0]` stand for "no houses" with no special case. Each entry needs only the two before it, so two variables could replace the vector. Only `best[1] = houses[0]` assumes a house exists, so the example's `robLine` returns 0 for an empty street first. "The best with this item or without it" solves many problems: write down what each choice leaves you with, and keep the better one.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

long long robLine(const std::vector<int>& houses) {
    if (houses.empty()) {
        return 0;
    }
    int n = static_cast<int>(houses.size());
    std::vector<long long> best(n + 1, 0);
    best[1] = houses[0];
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) {
        best[i] = std::max(best[i - 1], best[i - 2] + houses[i - 1]);
    }
    return best[n];
}

int main() {
    int q = 0;
    std::cin >> q;
    for (int t = 0; t < q; ++t) {
        int n = 0;
        std::cin >> n;
        std::vector<int> houses(n);
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            std::cin >> houses[i];
        }
        std::cout << "best " << robLine(houses) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

