}

steps.body:
Coin change: what is the fewest coins that add up to an amount? Grabbing the biggest coin first can fail. With coins 1, 3, and 4, the amount 6 is 3 + 3, not 4 + 1 + 1. Instead, build a table of the fewest coins for every amount from 0 up. For amount `a`, try each coin `c` as the last one used: it leaves `a - c`, whose answer is already in the table:

```cpp
std::vector<int> coins = {1, 3, 4};
int amount = 6;
const int INF = amount + 1;             // more coins than any real answer
std::vector<int> dp(amount + 1, INF);
dp[0] = 0;                              // no coins make 0
for (int a = 1; a <= amount; ++a) {
    for (int c : coins) {
        if (c <= a && dp[a - c] != INF) {
            dp[a] = std::min(dp[a], dp[a - c] + 1);
        }
    }
}
std::cout << (dp[amount] == INF ? -1 : dp[amount]) << '\n';   // 2
```

The sentinel `INF` marks amounts that can't be made yet. `amount + 1` works because no answer needs more coins than the amount itself, and it leaves room to add 1. `INT_MAX` is riskier: drop the `!= INF` check and `INT_MAX + 1` overflows, and the negative result would win every `std::min`. The table costs O(amount times the number of coins), and one table answers every amount up to its size.

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::vector<int> coins(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::cin >> coins[i];
    }
    int q = 0;
    std::cin >> q;
    std::vector<int> amounts(q);
    for (int i = 0; i < q; ++i) {
        std::cin >> amounts[i];
    }

    int largest = *std::max_element(amounts.begin(), amounts.end());
    const int INF = largest + 1;
    std::vector<int> dp(largest + 1, INF);
    dp[0] = 0;
    for (int a = 1; a <= largest; ++a) {
        for (int c : coins) {
            if (c <= a && dp[a - c] != INF) {
                dp[a] = std::min(dp[a], dp[a - c] + 1);
            }
        }
    }

    for (int a : amounts) {
        std::cout << a << ": " << (dp[a] == INF ? -1 : dp[a]) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

